WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.912
Divisions aren't the only way to cut DDI.

2
00:00:02.912 --> 00:00:06.680
Many agencies split it
by service instead: DNS,

3
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.538
DHCP and IPAM teams, each
working across the whole agency.

4
00:00:11.538 --> 00:00:16.137
NIOS supports that with the same permission
model, but each service has a catch.

5
00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:20.275
This is an illustrative design,
not a validated configuration.

6
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:21.862
First, the rules.

7
00:00:21.862 --> 00:00:23.963
Everything is denied by default.

8
00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:27.344
A global permission covers
every object of a type,

9
00:00:27.344 --> 00:00:32.787
so read-write on all DHCP networks also
covers their ranges and fixed addresses.

10
00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:38.663
An object permission covers one member,
zone, network, record or fixed address.

11
00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:43.116
Inheritance runs parent to child, a
more specific permission overrides,

12
00:00:43.116 --> 00:00:48.163
and the first match wins, with the group's
own permissions checked before its roles.

13
00:00:48.163 --> 00:00:52.467
The DNS team gets read-write
on its DNS views and zones,

14
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:57.163
per record type if needed, and
Member DNS on its DNS members.

15
00:00:57.163 --> 00:00:58.538
No network rights.

16
00:00:58.538 --> 00:01:02.676
Note that response policy zone
rules only take global permissions.

17
00:01:02.676 --> 00:01:05.363
The DHCP team is where the catch is.

18
00:01:05.363 --> 00:01:09.401
A permission on a network covers
its ranges and fixed addresses,

19
00:01:09.401 --> 00:01:10.849
so don't grant networks.

20
00:01:10.849 --> 00:01:15.114
Grant object permissions on
the ranges and fixed addresses,

21
00:01:15.114 --> 00:01:18.525
plus Member DHCP and
restart on the DHCP members.

22
00:01:18.525 --> 00:01:23.837
The IPAM team gets read-write on network
containers and networks to allocate space.

23
00:01:23.837 --> 00:01:27.576
That inherits down to
ranges and fixed addresses,

24
00:01:27.576 --> 00:01:32.863
so if the DHCP team must own them, add
an explicit deny on the DHCP ranges.

25
00:01:32.863 --> 00:01:36.004
For contrast, a division
admin gets all three

26
00:01:36.004 --> 00:01:39.062
services inside one
slice of address space.

27
00:01:39.062 --> 00:01:40.750
Two points stay Grid-wide.

28
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.983
Turn on DNS Object Permissions
in Networks and Ranges, and A,

29
00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:50.512
AAAA and PTR records and hosts need
read-write on both the zone and the network.

30
00:01:50.512 --> 00:01:54.813
That's a deliberate check
between the DNS and IPAM teams.

31
00:01:54.813 --> 00:02:00.624
And only superusers create groups, roles,
attributes and approval workflows.

32
00:02:00.624 --> 00:02:04.513
Sources: Infoblox NIOS: About
Administrative Permissions;

